---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Library Card signup CTA. The signup form is out of scope for v3 — using a
// mailto placeholder that lands in the Library inbox with a structured
// subject line. The mailto avoids a "coming soon" badge that would dampen
// reader interest and routes serious inquiries to staff.
const LIBRARY_CARD_MAILTO =
  'mailto:library@schooltrusts.net?subject=Library%20Card%20signup';

// Doors carried over from the retired schooltrusts.org /help page, which now
// redirects here. Same structured-subject convention as the Library Card
// mailto above, routed to the institutional inbox rather than to a person.
const DOCUMENT_GIFT_MAILTO =
  'mailto:library@schooltrusts.net?subject=A%20document%20for%20the%20Library';
const BOARD_SERVICE_MAILTO =
  'mailto:library@schooltrusts.net?subject=Board%20or%20Law%20Librarian%20service';

// Site Update v9, Super-agent GAMMA, C.1 — four-question role-frame rewrite.
// Each role answers four questions concretely:
//   1. What public office are you occupying?
//   2. What is the smallest useful act?
//   3. What artifact will you leave behind?
//   4. What standing do you receive?
// Pseudonymity tier preserved verbatim from the prior /contribute/ page (see
// the "Pseudonymity" section below).
const roles = [
  {
    name: 'Reader',
    commitment: 'No signup required',
    office:
      "An ordinary citizen using a public reference library. Margaret's line — “Most people don’t realize they are the beneficiaries of this trust” — applies most directly here. As a reader you are exercising the most basic civic standing: knowing what is yours.",
    act:
      'Read one source page in the <a href="/reading/">Reading Room</a> or one state page in the <a href="/atlas/">Atlas</a>, and follow the citation back to the primary document. If you find an error or have a source to suggest, submit a correction — you do not have to become a member to do that.',
    artifact:
      'A correction or source suggestion in the Library’s public record. Even unaccepted submissions are logged; accepted ones credit the next more-accurate version of the page.',
    standing:
      'The standing of any member of the public who has read the record. No byline, no card — but the right to ask the next, harder question.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Library Card holder',
    commitment: 'Free signup',
    office:
      'A registered patron of the Library. The card identifies you (real name or, where role conflict requires it, pseudonym verified by the Library Board) and gives you a place to stand inside the institution.',
    act:
      'Sign up: email, state, role, and one line about what brought you here. After that, the easiest contribution is a correction. Read a source page, find an error, submit it.',
    artifact:
      'Your name (or pseudonym) on the Library’s patron rolls; the <a href="/newsroom/">Newsroom</a> in your inbox; the standing to submit corrections, annotations, and source suggestions for the Founders’ Library and the Scholarship section.',
    standing:
      'The patron tier. The entry tier for everyone who wants to be in the conversation rather than only watching it.',
    cta: { href: LIBRARY_CARD_MAILTO, label: 'Get a Library Card' },
  },
  {
    name: 'Contributor',
    commitment: 'Issue-by-issue',
    office:
      'A Library Card holder whose corrections, annotations, or source suggestions have been accepted into the editorial summaries. The office is conferred page by page, not in advance.',
    act:
      'Submit a substantive correction or annotation that survives editorial review. Suggest a primary source the Library is missing. Each accepted submission is one act of contribution.',
    artifact:
      'A named or pseudonymous credit on the source page you contributed to. The credit is durable: it stays with the page across future revisions, with version history.',
    standing:
      'A public byline on a public reference work. The same kind of standing a copy-editor or research assistant earns at any serious editorial institution.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Title Steward',
    commitment: 'A few hours a year per title',
    office:
      'A community curator of a specific in-copyright reference work in the Scholarship section. Especially well-suited to law-school faculty teaching trust law, trust-and-estates attorneys with Westlaw access, school-finance officers, state-lands-agency staff, and library-science curators with institutional access to the title.',
    act:
      'Refine the Library’s stub summary of a single title into something authoritative. Renewable annually; bounded — a few hours a year per title.',
    artifact:
      'The title’s editorial summary, with your byline as the maintaining steward. Open titles include Bogert’s “Law of Trusts and Trustees,” the Restatement (Third) of Trusts, the NEFA “Funding Public Schools in the United States and Indian Country” (2024), Souder & Fairfax 1996, and Culp et al. 2006.',
    standing:
      'Named curator of a reference-work entry in a public library. The most actively recruited role in the Library at present.',
    cta: { href: '/reading/scholarship/#tier-3', label: 'See open titles' },
  },
  {
    name: 'State Correspondent',
    commitment: 'Light, ongoing',
    office:
      'A Library Card holder who watches school-trust news in their own state and reports what they see. The closest analogue to Margaret’s description of the regular citizen who shows up, asks the question, and refuses to look away.',
    act:
      'Submit Newsroom-eligible items from your state — board votes, budget moves, audit releases, court filings, agency turnover. One item is enough to start.',
    artifact:
      'Newsroom items credited to you (or your pseudonym). A state-specific Newsroom feed delivered to your inbox.',
    standing:
      'The named correspondent for your state. Light commitment, but a public office: when the Library cites a Newsroom item from your state, your name is on it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'State Co-Librarian',
    commitment: '5–10 hours a month after onboarding',
    office:
      'The named curator of one state’s corner of the State Records section. The substantive expertise on each state’s school trust lives in the agency that manages those lands day-to-day; the Co-Librarian role brings that expertise into the Library. Primary recruit pool: state-lands-agency staff. Pseudonymity tier available for staff whose participation under their real name could be professionally costly.',
    act:
      'Curate the per-state record — enabling act, original constitutional education clauses, key state Supreme Court decisions, period audit reports, AG opinions, reform-era statutes — and validate, supplement, and correct what the Library says about your state.',
    artifact:
      'A maintained state corner of the State Records section, with your byline (or pseudonym) as the institutional steward.',
    standing:
      'Named co-librarian of a state corner of a public library. The role is scarce — one per state — and carries the institutional standing of the agency or expertise the Co-Librarian represents.',
    cta: { href: '/contribute/state-lands-agencies/', label: 'For state-lands-agency staff →' },
  },
  {
    name: 'Library Board',
    commitment: 'Governance',
    office:
      'A trustee of the Library itself. Separate from the operating boards of ASTL and OASTL, the Library Board provides editorial guidance, archival discipline, and the institutional memory of how a library posture differs from an advocacy posture.',
    act:
      'Approve annual additions to the Founders’ Library and the Scholarship section. Arbitrate editorial disputes. Review retraction-and-correction protocols. Hold the Library to its own stated method.',
    artifact:
      'Board minutes, annual editorial decisions, and the durable governance record of a public reference institution.',
    standing:
      'Library trustee. The first three to five Board members are being recruited from library-science, archival, and digital-library-infrastructure backgrounds.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to contribute · America's School Trust Library"
  description="Reader, Library Card holder, Contributor, Title Steward, State Correspondent, State Co-Librarian, Library Board — seven roles, ordered from lightest to heaviest commitment, each with defined operational task and institutional standing."
  sourceId="contribute-landing"
>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">Contribute</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2">How to contribute</h1>
    <p class="kicker text-slate-600">
      The Library grows through readers, contributors, and curators.
    </p>

    <div class="margaret-pull-quote">
      <p>This is a delightfully non-partisan issue. It doesn't matter what party you're in, or if you're an independent, you still love your children, your grandchildren, and the other children that are all going to grow up, be workers, and be paying your social security.</p>
      <p class="attribution">— Margaret Bird, video transcript on file (August 2025).</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What the Library is</h2>
    <p>
      America's School Trust Library is a public reference library for the trust lands
      and trust funds the United States set aside for public schools — beginning with
      Section 16 of every township in 1785, expanded over two and a half centuries,
      and now scattered across the records of fifty state agencies. The Library is
      the institution that makes that record findable. For the longer entry-door
      read, see <a href="/why/">Why this matters</a>. A 20-page portrait of the
      Library — <a href="/booklet/">The Library in brief</a> — is available as a
      free PDF.
    </p>
  </article>

  {/* C.2 — Watchdog opening paragraph. Inserted above the role cards.
      Margaret quotations are verbatim, marked as quotations, and attributed
      to the editorial substrate as evidence. The Library does not yet
      surface the Margaret transcripts at a public path; until that lands,
      attribution is by footnote rather than a broken link. */}
  <section class="watchdog-opening not-prose mt-10 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-slate-700 bg-slate-50 p-5 shadow-sm">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-700">
      The watchdog citizen
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
      What contributors are actually doing
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base text-slate-800">
      The Library exists for a constituency that does not yet know it is one. As
      Margaret Bird puts it: <q class="italic">Most people don’t realize they
      are the beneficiaries of this trust.</q> The school-trust corpus was set
      aside in 1785 for the schoolchildren of the Republic in perpetuity, and
      that beneficiary class — every public-school child in every land-grant
      state, plus the watchdog citizens, school-board members, journalists,
      and lawyers who hold the trust on their behalf — has rarely been told.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base text-slate-800">
      Telling them, and helping them act, is what the seven roles below are for.
      Margaret’s second line names what the act looks like: <q class="italic">A
      regular citizen can be a watchdog. … When parents and citizens speak up,
      the legislators listen.</q> The Library’s job is to make speaking up
      cheap, accurate, and durable. The contributor’s job — whether you arrive
      as a watchdog citizen, a school-board member, a journalist, or a
      lawyer — is to speak up.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs italic text-slate-600">
      Margaret Bird, video transcripts on file with the Library (held in the
      editorial substrate — see <a href="/about/how-this-works/#substrate">How
      this works · Substrate</a> — 2025–2026). The transcripts are being
      prepared for public release as one of the open Library missions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="hero-role not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-amber-500 bg-amber-50 p-5 shadow-sm">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-900">
      Most actively recruited role
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-amber-950">
      Title Steward
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-amber-950">
      A community curator who has institutional access to a specific in-copyright
      reference work in the Scholarship section, and who refines the Library's stub
      summary into something authoritative. Renewable annually. Bounded — a few
      hours a year per title. Open titles include Bogert's <em>Law of Trusts and
      Trustees</em>, the <em>Restatement (Third) of Trusts</em>, the NEFA
      <em>Funding Public Schools in the United States and Indian Country</em> (2024),
      Souder &amp; Fairfax 1996, and Culp et&nbsp;al. 2006.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      <a
        href="/reading/scholarship/#tier-3"
        class="inline-block rounded-md bg-amber-600 px-3 py-2 font-semibold text-white shadow hover:bg-amber-700"
      >See open titles &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="library-card-cta not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border border-slate-300 bg-white p-5 shadow-sm">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">First step</p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Get a Library Card</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
      The simplest first step is a free Library Card — email, state, role, and one
      line about what brought you here. After that, the easiest contribution is a
      correction: read a source page, find an error, submit it. Accepted corrections
      produce named or pseudonymous credit on the source page.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      <a
        href={LIBRARY_CARD_MAILTO}
        class="inline-block rounded-md bg-blue-700 px-3 py-2 font-semibold text-white shadow hover:bg-blue-800"
      >Get a Library Card</a>
      <span class="ml-2 text-xs italic text-slate-500">
        (placeholder during signup-form bring-up; emails go to library staff)
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="librarian-corps-cta not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 p-5 shadow-sm">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-blue-900">Build the Library</p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">From contributor to librarian</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
      The Library's people ladder is now explicit: Library Card holder,
      Contributor, named librarian. Named librarians hold bounded offices such
      as legal reference, state collections, corrections, cataloging, and future
      science review.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      <a href="/librarians/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 underline">Read the Librarian Corps program</a>
      <span class="mx-2 text-slate-400">·</span>
      <a href="/for-lawyers/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 underline">Legal on-ramp for lawyers</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="roles mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">The roles we offer</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm italic text-slate-600">
      Seven roles, ordered from lightest to heaviest commitment. Each role answers
      four questions: what public office you are occupying, what the smallest useful
      act is, what artifact you will leave behind, and what standing the Library
      confers in return. Named or, where role conflicts require it, pseudonymous.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {roles.map((r) => (
        <li class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-sm">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">{r.commitment}</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">{r.name}</h3>
          <dl class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
            <div>
              <dt class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">
                Public office
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1">{r.office}</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">
                Smallest useful act
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1" set:html={r.act}></dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">
                Artifact you leave behind
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1" set:html={r.artifact}></dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-slate-500">
                Standing you receive
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-1">{r.standing}</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          {r.cta && (
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
              <a href={r.cta.href} class="font-semibold text-blue-700 underline">
                {r.cta.label}
              </a>
            </p>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <article class="prose prose-slate mt-12 max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <h2>Contribute documents</h2>
    <p>
      Not every contribution is a role. The collection grows by gift, and
      attics matter as much as archives: old agency reports, school-board
      minutes, lease and sale records, maps, letters, photographs, newspaper
      clippings — public-record material from any of the school-trust states.
      The Library preserves what you send, records where it came from, and
      makes it findable for every reader after you. The provenance note is the
      artifact you leave behind, and it stays with the document. If you are not
      sure whether something belongs, describe it rather than send it, and the
      Library will tell you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={DOCUMENT_GIFT_MAILTO}>Write to the Library about a document &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Report an error</h2>
    <p>
      Public correction is one of the Library's founding promises, and since
      August 3, 2026 it is an adopted one: the
      <a href="/rights/">Constitution and Readers' Bill of Rights</a> devotes an
      article to correction, challenge, and appeal, and gives every reader the
      right to visible correction. If anything on these shelves looks wrong — a
      figure, a date, a citation, a characterization — say so. Material errors
      are fixed, the fix is made in public, and the reader who caught it has
      done the Library a service. No Library Card is required.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/correct/">Report an error &rarr;</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; The protocol
      is at <a href="/about/corrections/">Corrections</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Pseudonymity</h2>
    <p>
      For contributors whose role might create employer or political conflict, the
      Library offers a pseudonymity tier. Verification happens internally: real
      name, employer, and contact information are held confidentially by the
      Library Board's verification committee. The public-facing standing — the
      byline on a source page, the Newsroom contributor credit, the State
      Co-Librarian designation — accrues to the pseudonym. This is essential for
      the recruitment of state-lands-agency staff and others whose participation
      under their real name could be professionally costly.
    </p>

    <h2>Serving as a director or as Law Librarian</h2>
    <p>
      America's School Trust Library is an Oregon nonprofit public benefit
      corporation, and its board of directors — the three people named on the
      <a href="/about/">About</a> page — was seated August 3, 2026 and adopted
      the Library's governing documents by unanimous written consent.
      <strong>Two director seats are open now.</strong> Under the
      <a href="/bylaws/">Bylaws</a> the board may have between three and eleven
      directors, and it intends to grow to five and then toward seven, with
      particular attention to education, libraries and archives, finance, and
      beneficiary communities. Directors serve one-year terms, serve without
      compensation, and sign the annual disclosure in the
      <a href="/governance/">Conflict of Interest Policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>Law Librarian</strong> seat is open as well. The bylaws make it
      a professional role rather than an officer or director seat: the Law
      Librarian stewards the legal collection under a written charter covering
      collection scope, cataloging, citation verification, public legal
      information, and the separation of public Library work from privileged,
      confidential, or active-litigation work. The Library provides legal
      information, never legal advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no application form and no deadline, and it would be dishonest
      to invent one. Write to the Library, say which seat interests you and
      what you would bring to it, and the board will answer. Filling these
      seats is not housekeeping: the adopted conflict-of-interest policy names
      the growth of the board toward a disinterested majority as an important
      safeguard in its own right.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={BOARD_SERVICE_MAILTO}>Write to the Library about a board or Law Librarian seat &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't ask</h2>
    <p>
      No copyright transfer (corrections and annotations are licensed CC BY 4.0 by
      default; primary-source contributions are public-domain dedications). No
      political alignment — the Library is a fiduciary-data project, not an
      advocacy outlet, and accepts contributions from anyone with the accuracy and
      good faith to do the work. No commitment beyond what you choose. No fees.
    </p>

    <h2>About donations</h2>
    <p>
      The Library is not asking you for money, and that is deliberate: it asks
      for documents and hands before dollars. The paperwork that authorizes a
      nonprofit to invite donations is done — Oregon Department of Justice
      Charitable Activities Registration No. 72571 was completed July 10, 2026,
      and the corporation was formed July 21, 2026 — so this is a choice about
      building the right way, not a legal barrier. The Library would rather owe
      its early shape to contributors than to donors. The adopted
      <a href="/rights/">Constitution and Readers' Bill of Rights</a> states the
      same principle from the reader's side: access to the public record is not
      conditioned on an account, a donation, or an affiliation.
    </p>
    <p>
      So there is no donate button on this page, and no payment link. One
      honest limit remains as well: no federal tax-exemption determination has
      been issued, and until one is, the Library does not describe
      contributions as tax-deductible. When the Library begins accepting
      support, this page will say so plainly. Until then, the contributions it
      actually needs are the ones listed above — a correction, a document, a
      title, a state, a seat.
    </p>

    <h2>A note for state-lands-agency staff</h2>
    <p>
      If you work for a State Department of Lands, a State Land Board, the Utah
      School and Institutional Trust Lands Administration (SITLA), Washington's
      DNR, or any of the other thirty-plus state agencies that administer school
      trust lands — please read the
      <a href="/contribute/state-lands-agencies/">dedicated page for state-lands-agency staff</a>.
      The lands you administer were set aside in 1785 for the maintenance of
      public schools, and the Library is the institution that makes that record
      findable to the country. Your professional familiarity with those records is
      the highest-value contribution this Library can receive.
    </p>

    <hr class="my-10" />

    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
      <em>Curated by Library editorial team, 2026-05-08; updated 2026-08-09.</em>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
